# SGOT UI/UX Design System

SGOT is an internal operational application. Its interface must feel professional,
calm, modern, efficient and information-dense without becoming visually heavy.

The visual direction is inspired by high-quality productivity applications such as
Linear, but SGOT must maintain its own identity. Do not copy another product's
interface pixel-for-pixel.

## Core design principles

- Prioritize clarity, usability and information hierarchy over decoration.
- Keep the interface visually calm and consistent.
- Reduce unnecessary visual noise.
- Use whitespace intentionally.
- Prefer subtle borders and surface contrast over heavy shadows.
- Important operational information must be easy to scan.
- Actions must have an obvious hierarchy.
- Do not sacrifice readability for visual effects.
- Keep interactions predictable across the entire application.

## Typography

Use Inter Variable as the primary SGOT typeface.

Preferred font stack:

`"Inter Variable", "Inter", system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif`

Typography should use a limited and intentional set of weights.

Recommended use:

- 400: normal body text
- 450–500: UI labels and secondary emphasis
- 550–600: buttons, navigation and important labels
- 600–650: headings

Avoid excessive bold text.

Use tabular numbers where useful for:
- quantities
- OT numbers
- dates where alignment benefits readability
- reports and numeric tables

Do not use multiple decorative font families.

## Themes

SGOT must support two first-class visual themes:

1. Light
2. Classic Dark

Both themes must have the same hierarchy, spacing and component behavior.

Do not build the dark theme by simply inverting the light theme.

Define theme values through reusable CSS custom properties.

Examples of semantic tokens:

- `--bg-app`
- `--bg-sidebar`
- `--bg-surface`
- `--bg-surface-elevated`
- `--bg-hover`
- `--border-default`
- `--border-subtle`
- `--text-primary`
- `--text-secondary`
- `--text-muted`
- `--accent`
- `--accent-hover`
- `--success`
- `--warning`
- `--danger`
- `--info`
- `--shadow-sm`
- `--shadow-md`

Components must use semantic variables instead of hard-coded light/dark colors.

A visual component should not need separate markup for light and dark themes.

## Color

Use a restrained neutral palette.

SGOT should use color primarily for:

- status
- feedback
- primary actions
- selected navigation
- meaningful operational information

Do not use bright colors merely as decoration.

Status colors must remain understandable in both themes.

Never rely on color alone to communicate critical state.

## Layout

Use a persistent application shell consisting of:

- sidebar navigation
- top/application header where useful
- main content area

The sidebar should be visually quieter than the main working area.

The current section must be immediately recognizable.

Keep content widths comfortable on large displays while allowing data tables to
use the available horizontal space.

Avoid excessive empty space in operational screens.

## Spacing

Use a consistent spacing scale instead of arbitrary values.

Preferred rhythm:

- 4px
- 8px
- 12px
- 16px
- 20px
- 24px
- 32px
- 40px

Do not introduce random margins or padding when an existing spacing token can be
used.

## Borders and radius

Use restrained border radii.

Recommended hierarchy:

- inputs / small controls: 6–8px
- buttons: 6–8px
- cards / panels: 10–12px
- overlays / dropdowns: 10–14px

Avoid excessive pill-shaped components except where appropriate for:
- status badges
- filters
- compact selectors

Borders should normally be subtle.

## Glassmorphism

Glassmorphism is an accent technique, not the base SGOT visual language.

It may be used for contextual floating surfaces such as:

- dropdown menus
- command/search panels
- popovers
- floating filter panels
- modals
- compact contextual action menus

Typical treatment may include:

- translucent surface
- `backdrop-filter: blur(...)`
- subtle border
- soft shadow

Do NOT use glassmorphism for:

- primary page backgrounds
- large content cards
- data tables
- long forms
- normal information panels

Always provide sufficient opaque fallback/background contrast.

Readability and accessibility take priority over transparency effects.

## Cards and surfaces

Cards should be used to group related information, not merely to decorate the
page.

Prefer:

- subtle surface contrast
- light border
- clear heading hierarchy

Avoid stacking many nested cards.

Important actions may appear in a card header when they apply to the entire
section.

For example, actions such as:

- Registrar devoluciones
- Conciliar salida
- Programar
- Completar orden

should normally be visually grouped as actions rather than displayed as ordinary
data fields.

## Tables

Operational tables are a primary SGOT component.

Tables must prioritize scanning and density.

Use:

- clear but subtle column headers
- adequate row height
- subtle row separators
- hover state
- consistent numeric alignment
- status badges
- responsive horizontal scrolling when necessary

Do not make every table cell visually boxed.

Numeric columns should use tabular numbers where useful.

Dates shown to users should be human-readable and consistent.

Avoid exposing internal enum values such as:

- `RECEIVED`
- `EQUIPMENT_OK`
- `IN_INSTALLATION`

when a human-readable Spanish label exists.

## Forms

Forms must have:

- clear labels
- consistent input heights
- visible focus state
- disabled state
- validation state
- concise help/error messages

Do not depend only on placeholder text as a label.

Primary and secondary actions must be visually distinguishable.

Dangerous or irreversible actions require appropriate visual distinction and,
when necessary, confirmation.

## Buttons

Use a small, consistent button hierarchy:

- primary
- secondary
- subtle/ghost
- danger

Do not create a new button style for each page.

Buttons with the same purpose must look the same across SGOT.

Use icons only when they improve recognition.

Avoid decorative icons that add no information.

## Status badges

Statuses should use compact badges with semantic styling.

Examples:

- Recibida
- Pendiente de equipos
- Equipos OK
- En instalación
- Completada
- Pendiente de conciliación
- Conciliada

Use human-readable Spanish labels in the UI.

Internal enum/database values must remain implementation details.

## Navigation

Navigation must clearly indicate:

- current section
- hover state
- available actions

Use consistent iconography.

Do not overcrowd the sidebar.

Navigation labels should use business terminology understood by SGOT users.

## Feedback and empty states

Success, warning and error feedback must be visible but not visually aggressive.

Empty states should explain:

- what is missing
- whether the user can take an action
- what that action is

Avoid generic empty messages when more useful context is available.

## Responsive behavior

The application is desktop-first because SGOT is primarily an operational system,
but it must remain usable on narrower screens.

Do not assume a fixed desktop width.

Tables may scroll horizontally when necessary.

Forms should collapse logically into fewer columns.

Navigation must not cover content at smaller sizes.

## Interaction states

Every interactive component should consider:

- default
- hover
- focus
- active
- disabled
- loading when applicable
- validation/error where applicable

Keyboard focus must remain visible.

## Accessibility

Maintain sufficient text/background contrast.

Do not communicate status solely through color.

Use semantic HTML where practical.

Inputs must have labels.

Buttons and links must remain distinguishable.

Focus indicators must not be removed without an accessible replacement.

## CSS architecture

Prefer reusable CSS classes and semantic design tokens.

Do not use inline styles unless there is a compelling technical reason.

Avoid duplicated CSS.

Do not introduce page-specific arbitrary colors if an existing semantic token
fits the purpose.

Keep global visual primitives in the SGOT stylesheet/design system and keep
page-specific CSS minimal.

## Bootstrap

Bootstrap may continue to provide basic layout utilities where already useful.

Do not allow default Bootstrap visual styling to define SGOT's identity.

SGOT's custom design system should control:

- colors
- typography
- surfaces
- buttons
- forms
- tables
- badges
- navigation

Do not introduce another large UI framework without an explicit architectural
decision.

## JavaScript and themes

Theme switching must remain lightweight.

Prefer a small native JavaScript implementation.

The selected theme should persist between page loads.

Avoid introducing a frontend framework solely for theme switching or visual
effects.

Avoid flashes of the wrong theme during page load.

## Business logic separation

Visual refactors must not change business rules.

Do not move business logic into templates or JavaScript.

Django views/services/models remain responsible for application behavior.

A UI redesign must preserve:

- permissions
- workflow rules
- form validation
- service-layer operations
- URLs unless intentionally changed
- existing tests

When modifying existing screens for visual reasons, make the smallest necessary
HTML/CSS/JS changes.

## Visual consistency requirement

Before creating a new visual pattern, check whether SGOT already has a component
that solves the same problem.

Prefer improving and reusing an existing component over introducing a second
version.

The application should feel like one coherent product, not a collection of pages
designed independently.